window.__labLoaded({"id":"7.1","title":"Chi-Square Tests for Categories","goal":"Test whether ad type (video or banner) is related to buying, using the lesson's 200-person table, and measure the size of the link with Cramér's V.","question":"A marketing analyst at an online shop must choose which ad to run next month. She showed a video ad to 100 visitors and a banner ad to 100 others, and noted who bought: 60 of the video group and 45 of the banner group. Is ad type really related to buying, or could a 15-point gap like this appear by chance alone?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the table of counts","text":"Rows are the two ads and columns are bought or not. A chi-square test works on counts like these, never on percentages. Totals first, so you can see where the expected counts will come from.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\n# rows: video ad, banner ad    columns: bought, did not buy\nobserved = np.array([[60, 40],\n                     [45, 55]])\n\nprint('row totals   :', observed.sum(axis=1))\nprint('column totals:', observed.sum(axis=0))\nprint('grand total  :', observed.sum())","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for arrays of numbers, under the short name np.","Load SciPy's statistics tools, which hold the chi-square test we need.","A comment that says which row and column is which, so the table stays readable later.","Store the counts as a 2 by 2 array. This first row is the video ad: 60 bought, 40 did not.","The second row is the banner ad. Chi-square works on counts like these, never on percentages.","Add across each row (axis=1) to get the group sizes. We expect 100 and 100.","Add down each column (axis=0) to see how many bought (105) and did not buy (95) overall.","The grand total is the number of people. We need it to work out the expected counts."],"meaning":"Each ad was shown to 100 people, and 105 of the 200 bought. If ad type did not matter, each group would have about 52 or 53 buyers. The real counts are 60 and 45, so there is a gap to explain.","out":"row totals   : [100 100]\ncolumn totals: [105  95]\ngrand total  : 200","figs":[]},{"title":"Run the chi-square test","text":"chi2_contingency works out the expected counts (what we would see if ad type and buying were unrelated), then the chi-square statistic, degrees of freedom and p-value. correction=False gives the plain formula used in the lesson.","code":"res = stats.chi2_contingency(observed, correction=False)\n\nprint('expected counts:')\nprint(res.expected_freq)\nprint(f'chi-square = {res.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'df         = {res.dof}')\nprint(f'p-value    = {res.pvalue:.4f}')","explain":["Run the test of independence. correction=False uses the plain formula from the lesson, and the result holds everything we print.","Print a heading so the numbers below are labelled.","Expected counts are what each cell would hold if ad type had no link to buying.","The chi-square statistic adds up how far the real counts sit from the expected ones.","Degrees of freedom, (rows - 1) x (columns - 1), tell us which chi-square curve to compare with.","The p-value is how often a gap this big would appear if the ad type made no difference."],"meaning":"If ad type had no link to buying, each ad would have 52.5 buyers and 47.5 non-buyers. Chi-square = 4.511 with 1 df gives p = 0.0337. That is below 0.05, so a gap this big would be unusual by luck alone. We have evidence of a link.","out":"expected counts:\n[[52.5 47.5]\n [52.5 47.5]]\nchi-square = 4.511\ndf         = 1\np-value    = 0.0337","figs":[]},{"title":"Check it by hand","text":"Expected = row total x column total / grand total. Each cell then adds (observed - expected)^2 / expected, and the four pieces sum to the same chi-square.","code":"rows = observed.sum(axis=1)\ncols = observed.sum(axis=0)\nn = observed.sum()\n\nexpected = np.outer(rows, cols) / n\npieces = (observed - expected) ** 2 / expected\n\nprint(pieces.round(3))\nprint(f'sum of pieces = {pieces.sum():.3f}')","explain":["Row totals again, because the hand calculation starts from them.","Column totals, the other ingredient of every expected count.","The grand total n, the number of people in the table.","Expected count = row total x column total / n. np.outer multiplies every row total by every column total.","For each cell compute (observed - expected) squared, divided by expected. Dividing makes a gap matter more when little was expected.","Show the four pieces, rounded to 3 decimals, to see which cells contribute most.","Their sum is the chi-square statistic, which should match scipy's answer."],"meaning":"Every cell is 7.5 away from its expected count, but the pieces are 1.071 (buyers) and 1.184 (non-buyers), because a gap counts for more where fewer were expected. The four pieces add to 4.511, the same chi-square as scipy gave.","out":"[[1.071 1.184]\n [1.071 1.184]]\nsum of pieces = 4.511","figs":[]},{"title":"Measure the effect with Cramér's V","text":"A small p-value says the link is probably real, not how big it is. Cramér's V = sqrt(chi-square / (N x (k - 1))), where k is the smaller of the number of rows and columns. It runs from 0 (no link) to 1 (perfect link).","code":"chi2 = res.statistic\nk = min(observed.shape)\nv = np.sqrt(chi2 / (n * (k - 1)))\n\nprint(f'Cramer V = {v:.3f}')\nprint('bought: video', observed[0, 0] / rows[0], ' banner', observed[1, 0] / rows[1])","explain":["Take the chi-square value from the test result so we can reuse it.","k is the smaller of the number of rows and columns. Here it is 2.","Cramer's V = square root of chi-square / (n x (k - 1)). Dividing by n removes the effect of sample size.","Print V to three decimals. 0 means no link and 1 means a perfect link.","Print the buying rate in each group (bought / group size) so the size of the gap is visible in plain terms."],"meaning":"V = 0.150 is a small effect (roughly 0.1 small, 0.3 medium, 0.5 large). In plain words, 60% of the video group bought against 45% of the banner group. The p-value says the link is probably real; V says it is modest, not huge.","out":"Cramer V = 0.150\nbought: video 0.6  banner 0.45","figs":[]},{"title":"Bonus: goodness of fit","text":"A goodness-of-fit test checks one set of counts against a claimed pattern. Here a die was rolled 120 times; chisquare assumes every face is equally likely unless you give it other expected counts.","code":"rolls = np.array([25, 17, 15, 23, 24, 16])\n\nfit = stats.chisquare(rolls)\n\nprint(f'expected per face = {rolls.sum() / 6:.0f}')\nprint(f'chi-square = {fit.statistic:.3f}, df = {len(rolls) - 1}')\nprint(f'p-value    = {fit.pvalue:.4f}')","explain":["Store how many times each of the six faces came up in 120 rolls of a die.","chisquare is the goodness-of-fit test. With no expected counts given, it assumes all six faces are equally likely.","The expected count per face is the total rolls divided by 6.","Print the statistic and its degrees of freedom, which are the number of categories minus 1.","Print the p-value: how surprising these counts would be if the die were fair."],"meaning":"A fair die would show each face 20 times in 120 rolls. Chi-square = 5.000 with 5 df gives p = 0.4159, far above 0.05. Counts like 25 and 15 are ordinary luck, so we have no evidence against a fair die. That is not proof it is fair.","out":"expected per face = 20\nchi-square = 5.000, df = 5\np-value    = 0.4159","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"ads <- matrix(c(60, 45, 40, 55), nrow = 2,\n              dimnames = list(ad = c(\"video\", \"banner\"), bought = c(\"yes\", \"no\")))\ntest <- chisq.test(ads, correct = FALSE)\ntest\ntest$expected\nsqrt(test$statistic / (sum(ads) * (min(dim(ads)) - 1)))\nchisq.test(c(25, 17, 15, 23, 24, 16))","expect":"X-squared = 4.5113, df = 1, p-value = 0.03365; expected counts 52.5, 47.5, 52.5, 47.5; Cramer's V = 0.150; die: X-squared = 5, df = 5, p-value = 0.4159","note":"correct = FALSE turns off the Yates continuity correction that R applies to 2 by 2 tables by default, so the numbers match the lesson and the Python. R has no built-in Cramer's V, so the last line computes it by hand.","explain":["Build the 2 by 2 table. c() lists the counts column by column: bought (60, 45) first, then did not buy (40, 55).","dimnames names the rows and columns so the printed table is easy to read.","chisq.test runs the test. correct = FALSE turns off Yates' correction so R matches the lesson and the Python.","Typing the name test prints the result: X-squared, df and the p-value.","test$expected prints the expected counts, what we would see if ad and buying were unrelated.","R has no built-in Cramer's V, so we compute it: square root of chi-square / (n x (k - 1)).","Given a plain vector of counts, chisq.test does the goodness-of-fit test, assuming equal chances for each face."],"read":"Look for X-squared = 4.5113, df = 1, p-value = 0.03365 in the first output: below 0.05, so ad type and buying look related. The expected counts are 52.5 and 47.5. The V line shows 0.150 (a small effect). The die test gives p = 0.4159, so no evidence of a loaded die."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Crosstabs (Statistics: Chi-square, Phi and Cramer's V; Cells: Expected)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / ad buy count.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 1 60  1 2 40  2 1 45  2 2 55\nEND DATA.\nVALUE LABELS ad 1 'video' 2 'banner' / buy 1 'bought' 2 'did not buy'.\nWEIGHT BY count.\nCROSSTABS\n  /TABLES=ad BY buy\n  /STATISTICS=CHISQ PHI\n  /CELLS=COUNT EXPECTED.","note":"Read the Pearson Chi-Square row (SPSS also prints a continuity-corrected row for 2 by 2 tables, ignore it here). Cramer's V is in the Symmetric Measures table. WEIGHT BY count tells SPSS each row stands for that many people.","explain":["Declare three variables to read in free format: the ad group, whether the person bought, and how many people that row stands for.","Marks the start of the data typed directly into the syntax.","Four rows of three numbers: ad code, buy code, count. For example 1 1 60 means video ad, bought, 60 people.","Marks the end of the inline data.","Give words to the codes so the output shows video and banner instead of 1 and 2.","Tell SPSS that each row counts as that many people, so it analyses 200 people, not 4 rows.","CROSSTABS builds a table of counts for two categorical variables.","Put ad in the rows and buy in the columns.","Ask for the chi-square test (CHISQ) and the measures of association PHI, which includes Cramer's V.","Show the observed count and the expected count in each cell. The full stop ends the command."],"read":"In Chi-Square Tests, read the Pearson Chi-Square row: value 4.511, df 1, Asymp. Sig. (2-sided) 0.034. Below 0.05, so ad type and buying are related. The Symmetric Measures table gives Cramer's V = 0.150, a small effect. The Crosstabulation shows expected counts of 52.5 and 47.5."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput ad buy freq\n1 1 60\n1 2 40\n2 1 45\n2 2 55\nend\ntabulate ad buy [fweight = freq], chi2 V expected","note":"tabulate with chi2 gives the Pearson chi-square, df and p-value; V adds Cramer's V; expected prints the expected counts. The goodness-of-fit bonus has no one-line built-in in Stata, so it is shown only in Python and R.","explain":["Clear any data already in memory so we start fresh.","Start typing data directly. Each row will hold three numbers: ad, buy and freq (the count).","Row 1: video ad (1), bought (1), 60 people.","Row 2: video ad (1), did not buy (2), 40 people.","Row 3: banner ad (2), bought (1), 45 people.","Row 4: banner ad (2), did not buy (2), 55 people.","end finishes the data entry.","tabulate crosses ad by buy. [fweight = freq] counts each row freq times, chi2 gives the test, V gives Cramer's V and expected shows expected counts."],"read":"Find the line Pearson chi2(1) = 4.5113 Pr = 0.034: the p-value is below 0.05, so ad type and buying are related. The line Cramer's V = 0.1502 says the effect is small. Each table cell shows the observed count above its expected count (52.5 or 47.5)."},"conclusion":"Yes, there is evidence that ad type is related to buying: 60% of video viewers bought against 45% of banner viewers (chi-square = 4.51, df = 1, p = 0.034), so video looks better. The link is modest (Cramer's V = 0.15). Caution: this shows a link, and only supports 'the video ad causes more buying' if people were shown the ads at random.","practice":{"scenario":"A clinic wants fewer missed appointments. It sends a text reminder to 100 patients and phones another 100 patients, then records who turned up. 70 of the text group and 58 of the phone group attended. Is the type of reminder related to attending?","data":"observed = [[70, 30], [58, 42]]   # rows: text, phone call   columns: attended, missed","tasks":["Run a chi-square test of independence (no continuity correction) and report the expected counts, chi-square, df and p-value.","At the 0.05 level, is reminder type related to attendance?","Compute Cramer's V and say how big the link is."],"hint":"Use the same two functions as the lab: chi2_contingency with correction=False, then V = sqrt(chi-square / (n x (k - 1))).","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nobserved = np.array([[70, 30],\n                     [58, 42]])\nres = stats.chi2_contingency(observed, correction=False)\nprint('expected:', res.expected_freq.round(1).tolist())\nprint(f'chi-square = {res.statistic:.3f}, df = {res.dof}, p = {res.pvalue:.4f}')\n\nv = np.sqrt(res.statistic / (observed.sum() * (min(observed.shape) - 1)))\nprint(f'Cramer V = {v:.3f}')\nprint('attend rates:', observed[:, 0] / observed.sum(axis=1))","answer":"Expected counts are 64 attending and 36 missing in each group. Chi-square = 3.125, df = 1, p = 0.0771, which is above 0.05, so we cannot say reminder type matters: a 12-point gap (70% against 58%) is plausible by chance with 100 patients per group. Cramer's V = 0.125 is a small effect. Not significant does not mean the reminders are equal; a larger study might settle it.","out":"expected: [[64.0, 36.0], [64.0, 36.0]]\nchi-square = 3.125, df = 1, p = 0.0771\nCramer V = 0.125\nattend rates: [0.7  0.58]","figs":[]}}});
